foreign  in the course of a single morning in a  dizzying period of about three hours  Peru experienced an attempted coup  d'etat the arrest of its president and  the swearing end of a successor to  re-establish constitutional order it was  perhaps the shortest and worst executed  coup attempt of all time however this  whole story goes far beyond Pedro  Castile because in reality this is the  story of Peru's political decline a  story of hatred confrontation disloyalty  and the ambition for power let's get  cracking  [Music]  what pushed former president Pedro  Castillo to carry out such an active  institutional subversion was he really  able to dissolve Congress why did his  attempt fail and perhaps the most  important question of all what exactly  is going on with Peruvian politics that  is going from bad to worse is Castile  the only one to blame well there's your  politic viewers in this video we're  going to answer all of these questions  but first we need to establish a few  things Pedro Castile a humble rural  school teacher from one of the poorest  areas of Peru with a strong  anti-establishment discourse and no  political or management expertise was  sworn in as president on 28th of July  2021.  foreign  however he barely held the presidency  for 495 full days that is less than a  year and a half  [Music]  in the 2021 presidential elections  Castillo went to the second round with  just 18.9 of the vote and managed to win  by a slim margin of just over 40 000 out  of the nearly 19 million votes cast he  won thanks to a runoff against Kiku  fujimori the daughter of the imprisoned  former president Alberto fujimori who  was rejected by half the country be that  as it may it seemed clear from the  beginning that despite his Grand  promises Castillo was going to have a  very very difficult time he is a  president who took office with a very  low level of support he did not have a  political party he had a hard time  putting together a cabinet the cabinet  has changed constantly and there has  been a constant power struggle with  Congress Flavia freidenberg political  scientist at the national autonomous  University of Mexico without political  experience without a clear plan and  faced with a head-on rejection by the  Parliament that little more than 16  months that Castile was at the Helm of  the country where a disaster and only  495 days this president had five  governments and 78 different ministers  appointments were made practically every  week only one person Roberto Sanchez  minister of foreign trade and tourism  managed to remain in office during all  of that time others were dismissed or  resigned amid scandals and  misunderstandings of all kinds some  portfolios as important and Peruvian  Politics as the ministry of the interior  of the Ministry of Agriculture have seen  seven and six ministers respectively  remember that this was in less than a  year and a half it's total Madness Pedro  Castile changed government more times  than he changed socks  [Music]  obviously with so much back and forth in  practice any government action was  impossible and that's not all on top of  this shift in context we have to add to  Congress that decided to make his life  impossible the incompetence and lack of  planning in a president who is  alternating between Marxist and  conservative supporters and as if that  were not enough a bunch of corruption  investigations that mired him even  further in fact in October 2022 we find  this news check this out the Peruvian  public prosecutor's office announces  president Pedro Castillo and accuses him  of leading a criminal organization the  prosecutor claims that the plot is  entrenched in the Government Palace with  the aim of monopolizing controlling and  directing Contracting processes to  obtain illicit profits in total Castile  his wife his sister-in-law and two  nephews were facing a total of six  separate investigations and to influence  peddling irregular Contracting bribery  and even plagiarism among other possible  crimes in short Pedro casio's term as  president has been a lengthy torture  both for himself and for the country as  a whole we were talking about nearly 200  political scandals of all kinds in less  than 500 Days and so visual politic  viewers this is how we arrive on the 7th  of December 2022 on the day which  Castile blew up his presidency but why  did he do it what exactly did it consist  of was it really as so many are  suggested an attempt to carry out a new  socialist revolution in Latin America  and why did this act of institutional  subversion fail well let's find out  [Music]  the non coup d'etat  [Music]  in only 16 months the Peruvian Congress  carried out three presidential vacancy  processes against Pedro Castile  the presidential vacancy which is  something like a vote of no confidence  because the mechanism that the Peruvian  Constitution provides to Congress for  deposing president for a range of  reasons among them moral incapacity  which of course can mean practically  anything in other words the parliament  has a free hand to pursue presidents at  an endless succession of motions of  senshin  [Music]  presidential  situations  Vista English well the fact is that the  first two attempts failed and the third  attempt which was to be voted on on  Wednesday the 7th of December was going  the same way however that same Wednesday  early in the morning something happened  [Music]  the former Chief of Staff for the  ministry of housing salatil marufo  testified before a congressional  investigative committee he stated that  both then housing Minister generir  Alvaro and himself made recurring  payments to both Castile and Castillo's  sister in exchange for keeping their  posts the money allegedly came from Sada  Gorey a real estate businesswoman and  was mainly used to pay bribes and  campaign for the president you know the  ministry of Housing and a real estate  entrepreneur you can join the dots  whether or not 